# Release done - record what was delivered

<!-- prism:release-done-contract:v1 -->

Use this skill when verified work has been delivered to its target, or the delivery failed. One confirmation writes a dated
release record (`knowledge/wiki/releases/REL-XXX.md`) and, with it, the Release rows of the features and bugs it names. A
feature whose apps are all released is `released`. The record says what was delivered where and with what outcome; it is
never rewritten.

## Usage

`$release-done F-XXX[,F-YYY] [BUG-XXX ...] [--return in-dev | --rollback REL-XXX | --redeploy REL-XXX]`

| Form | Action | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `F-XXX[,F-YYY] [BUG-XXX ...]` | `release-done` | Settles the pending (or failed) Release row of each app it releases as `released` or `failed`, ships the verified bugs it names, and writes one record. |
| `F-XXX --return in-dev` | `release-return-dev` | Records the failed delivery of apps and sends them back to development; their delivery, QA and Release rows move to Evidence history. |
| `--rollback REL-XXX` | `release-rollback` | Records that the current delivery of `REL-XXX` was rolled back. It writes the record alone. |
| `--redeploy REL-XXX` | `release-redeploy` | Records a redeploy after a rollback (`REL-XXX` is the rollback), or after a failed redeploy (`REL-XXX` is that record). It writes the record alone. |

## Read-only preflight

When available, use:

```text
prism wiki transition-preflight F-XXX [path] --action release-done --json
```

Accept it only with common envelope schema 1, command facts, capability version 3 and an action-specific `release-done`
surface, transition version 2 and a consistent snapshot. The Prism version alone does not prove support. Otherwise fall
back to direct-file checks. The preflight is copy-only and never authorizes a write.

## Supported actions

- `release-done`: `in-dev` + `dev`, `ready-for-qa` + `qa`, `in-qa` + `qa` or `ready-for-release` + `release` -> the minimum of
  the app stages: `released` + `none` once every active app is released. Each named app is `ready-for-release`
  (`app_stage_mismatch`).
- `release-return-dev`: the same sources -> `in-dev` + `dev`.
- `release-rollback` and `release-redeploy`: any source from `in-dev` to `released`; the feature pages do not change.
- A bug that is `verified` + `release` becomes `released` + `none` when every app of the bug is released.

## Evidence and confirmation

Read `knowledge/wiki/SCHEMA.md`, `knowledge/wiki/LIFECYCLE.md`, `knowledge/wiki/ACTIONS.md`, `status-board.md`, the complete
feature (criteria, Delivery evidence, QA verification, Release, Evidence history), every bug page of the feature,
`knowledge/wiki/SETTINGS.md` (the delivery target of each app), the API contract the Delivery evidence cites, and every
release record that names the same features, bugs or apps. The format of a record is in `knowledge/wiki/releases/_FORMAT.md`.

### A release (`release-done`)

The proposal:

- changes the authoritative Release row of each app it releases, in the column order
  `| App | Target | Version | Attempt | Outcome | Record | Basis |`: Target is the app's delivery target in
  `SETTINGS.md` as it is now, Version the artifact QA verified, Attempt `release-<n>` (one more than the number of release
  records that delivered the app for this feature, rollbacks and redeploys excluded), Outcome `released` or `failed`, Record
  the link `[REL-00n](../releases/REL-00n.md)` and Basis `checked` or `attested`. A `released` row keeps the target it was
  recorded with;
- adds or replaces staging rows when they are known: the Target is an environment of the app, the attempt `—`; they are
  informational;
- clears the `release` domain of each released app in `app-revalidation`, and nothing else;
- sets the feature's status and owner to the minimum over the app stages;
- may resolve open questions owned by `release`;
- adds the record `knowledge/wiki/releases/REL-00n.md`, where `n` is one more than the highest record number: the front matter
  `id`, `title`, `date` (the preview day), `operation: pending` (the board writes the operation that applies it), `outcome`
  (`released` when every row is released, `failed` when none is, `partial` otherwise), `features`, `bugs` and, for a retry, `retry-of`;
  a Delivery row for every (item, app) the proposal settles, with the same target, version, attempt, outcome and basis as the
  Release row and an Evidence cell (`release:`, `tag:`, `deployment:`, `store:` or `package:` and a URL or a path in the
  workspace; a commit or a pull request is not release evidence); and, for each delivered row that cites a contract, the
  verbatim contract page in `## Contracts` under `### F-XXX <app>`;
- for each bug it ships, changes the bug page: a Release row for each app of the bug in the same column order, whose Version is
  the bug's verification artifact, and `status: released` + `owner: none` when every app of the bug is released (otherwise the
  bug stays `verified`, and a later `release-done` retries it);
- for a bug that fixes an app of a feature that is already released, also updates the Release row of that app in the feature page:
  the same target, attempt and outcome, the bug's artifact as Version and the new record as Record.

A bug ships with its feature: a bug that is linked to a feature app that is not yet released goes in the same proposal as the
feature (`bug_release_requires_feature`). A bug with `feature: none`, or one whose feature app is already released, ships alone.
Every `verified` bug of the feature and a released app ships with the app (`verified_bug_not_included`).

The board refuses the release when any of these fails:

- the target resolves for each app (`delivery_target_missing` for an app of the `other` stack with no declared target), and the
  Version is the verified artifact (`release_version_not_verified`);
- QA still covers each app: every criterion has a passing row at its current revision on the delivered artifact, and no row of the
  attempt is `fail` or `blocked` (`qa_evidence_stale`);
- no bug of the feature that lists the app blocks it (`open_bug_blocks_release`);
- every row of one app and target in the record shares its Version and Outcome (`release_artifact_conflict`);
- the requirement of each released app depends only on features and apps that are `released`, counting those the same record
  releases (`dependency_not_released`);
- no feature or app revalidation is pending;
- an open question owned by any role except `release` that the proposal leaves open;
- the record's number is the next one (`release_sequence_invalid`; `release_id_taken` when it exists) and its rows are those of the
  pages (`release_record_mismatch`).

### A failed delivery that needs development (`release-return-dev`)

The feature page archives the Delivery evidence, QA verification and Release rows of the apps it returns (and every integration
row that names them) in one `### <today> - release-return-dev` Evidence history entry, as `qa-fail` does, and goes to `in-dev`;
their requirement pages become `in-progress` and an `implemented` contract `agreed`. The record lists the failed delivery of
those apps (outcome `failed`) and nothing else.

### A rollback (`release-rollback`)

`--rollback REL-XXX` names the current delivery of every (app, target) it rolls back: the highest-numbered record with a
`released` row for it, redeploys included (`rollback_target_invalid` otherwise). The record has `rollback-of: REL-XXX`, outcome
`rolled-back`, and a row `rolled-back` with attempt `—` for each item that delivery carried. The feature and bug pages do not
change: a row records its own delivery.

### A redeploy (`release-redeploy`)

`--redeploy REL-XXX` names the latest record for the app and target: a rollback of the current delivery, or a failed redeploy of it
(`app_stage_mismatch` otherwise). The record has `retry-of: REL-XXX` and rows with attempt `—` that deliver the version of the
current delivery (`redeploy_version_mismatch`), `released` or `failed`.

Show the complete write set: the feature pages, the bug pages, the record, the status board, the index and the log. Reread the
source and context immediately before confirmation and once again after it. Only after explicit confirmation may the rows, the
record, the status, the status board row and a `log.md` entry be written.

If a multi-file write is partial, report the exact observed changes and stop for fresh recovery; no transaction is implied.
Decline or cancel means no mutation. A release record says what was delivered, not what runs now: after a failed attempt or a
rollback the latest record for the app says what is known.

## Rules

- write-capable skill
- the target is filled from `SETTINGS.md` when the release is recorded; changing the settings affects only later releases
- one record per proposal, never rewritten
- write current-state pages: replace superseded content in place, state rationale as a current fact and keep history in `log.md` and the records; mark each claim `**Decided:**`, `**Observed:**`, `**Proposed:**` or `**Assumed:**` (gaps stay in the Open questions table) and link the evidence of every Decided and Observed claim: the processed intake item, a record or a URL (see Evidence labels in the wiki schema)

## Output behavior

Return:

- the record and its outcome
- the features and bugs settled, with the apps released or failed
- the status each feature now has
- the next step for a failed delivery: `$release-done` again for a retry, `--return in-dev`, or `--rollback`

## Error and stop conditions

- if a feature file does not exist, return a clean missing-feature response
- if QA, a bug, a dependency or a pending revalidation blocks the release, report the board's code and what to do next, and stop without writing
- if the user wants to cancel, stop without writing
